[17:24:40] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-8944f8667df3c281","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/_dev/GLB/stepwise-fifa-qa/context-pack/system/execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"739\" start=\"100\" end=\"249\" returned=\"150\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   100|  - §14.1 When to fan out (and when not)\n   101|  - §14.2 Worker contract (incl. §14.2.1 sharded progress)\n   102|  - §14.3 Coordinator contract (shard → dispatch → merge → sidecar last)\n   103|  - §14.4 Verify before use\n   104|  - §14.5 Fallback (no delegation capability → inline unit loop)\n   105|  - §14.6 Skill-level enforcement\n   106|- §15 — Deliverable upload safety (web-shaped deliverables cross a byte-scanning WAF; HTML/SVG/JS-emitting skills only)\n   107|  - §15.1 Recognising the failure (403 + `text/html` = the request never reached the app)\n   108|  - §15.2 Forbidden byte patterns (chained bare `alert(`, SQLi signatures — in code, mock data, or prose)\n   109|  - §15.3 Authoring rules (no native browser dialogs; describe payloads, don't reproduce them)\n   110|  - §15.4 Pre-delivery scan — mandatory gate before the §11 sidecar\n   111|  - §15.5 Optional live-firewall verification\n   112|\n   113|---\n   114|\n   115|\n   116|## Section files\n   117|\n   118|Non-universal sections now live as individual files under `execution-protocol/`.\n   119|Read only those your route selects (see the routing rule above); each is readable\n   120|standalone with its original § numbering intact. The **universal** sections (§2,\n   121|§10.5, §11 — required by every skill) remain inlined below.\n   122|\n   123|| § | Location |\n   124||---|----------|\n   125|| §1  Session Identity | `execution-protocol/01-session-identity.md` |\n   126|| §2  `_progress.json` | inline below (universal) |\n   127|| §3  IMPL-STATE | `execution-protocol/03-impl-state.md` |\n   128|| §4  Memory Bank | `execution-protocol/04-memory-bank.md` |\n   129|| §5  FIC recovery | `execution-protocol/05-fic-recovery.md` |\n   130|| §6  Dual-format | `execution-protocol/06-dual-format.md` |\n   131|| §7  REPAIR / §7.3 REBUILD | `execution-protocol/07-repair-mode.md` |\n   132|| §8  Deviations | `execution-protocol/08-deviations.md` |\n   133|| §9  Non-interactive | `execution-protocol/09-non-interactive.md` |\n   134|| §10 Section generation | `execution-protocol/10-section-generation.md` |\n   135|| §10.5 Tool discipline | inline below (universal) |\n   136|| §11 Output sidecar | inline below (universal) |\n   137|| §12 Delegated exploration | `execution-protocol/12-delegated-exploration.md` |\n   138|| §13 Code-location | `execution-protocol/13-code-location.md` |\n   139|| §14 Parallel fan-out | `execution-protocol/14-parallel-fanout.md` |\n   140|| §15 Deliverable upload safety | `execution-protocol/15-deliverable-upload-safety.md` |\n   141|\n   142|A skill's route (e.g. `§2 → §10 → §10.5 → §11`) resolves each §N via this table:\n   143|universal sections are already here; the rest are one file-read away. Existing\n   144|\"see execution-protocol.md §N\" citations in the 123 skills keep working through\n   145|this one redirect hop — no skill edits required.\n   146|\n   147|---\n   148|\n   149|## 2. _progress.json — Early Signal Write\n   150|\n   151|**Purpose:** Prevents the orchestrator coordinator from sending SIGINT to a running skill.\n   152|\n   153|Write this as the **FIRST file action** in any skill execution (before creating any other outputs):\n   154|\n   155|```json\n   156|{\n   157|  \"skill\": \"{skill-name}\",\n   158|  \"session_id\": \"{SESSION_ID}\",\n   159|  \"status\": \"RUNNING\",\n   160|  \"started_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC, e.g. 2026-08-15T13:21:37Z}\",\n   161|  \"completed_at\": null,\n   162|  \"total\": 0,\n   163|  \"completed\": 0,\n   164|  \"items\": []\n   165|}\n   166|```\n   167|\n   168|Write to: `{output_folder}/_progress.json`\n   169|\n   170|**Update cadence:**\n   171|- On FIC trigger (Section 5): `\"status\": \"COMPACTION_NEEDED\"`\n   172|- On completion: `\"status\": \"COMPLETED\"`, `\"completed_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC}\"`\n   173|- On failure: `\"status\": \"FAILED\"`, `\"completed_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC}\"`\n   174|\n   175|**UTC, not local time with a `Z` on it.** Read the real clock and convert. A local timestamp\n   176|labelled `Z` reads as a lie about when the work ran — Stepwise stamps its own state in true UTC,\n   177|so the two disagree by your offset and every reviewer sees fabricated evidence where there was\n   178|only a wrong clock. If you cannot get UTC, omit the field rather than guess.\n   179|\n   180|**Status vocabulary — closed set.** Every value carries distinct semantics; the set is\n   181|closed so a monitor can classify any tracker without knowing which skill wrote it.\n   182|\n   183|| status | meaning | live? |\n   184||---|---|---|\n   185|| `RUNNING` | step is executing | yes |\n   186|| `PARTIAL` | chunked step finished its chunk with work remaining; a later invocation resumes | yes |\n   187|| `COMPACTION_NEEDED` | FIC trigger (§5); awaiting compaction | yes |\n   188|| `COMPLETED` | all declared work done | no — terminal |\n   189|| `FAILED` | step could not complete | no — terminal |\n   190|| `SCOPE_REFUSED` | input exceeded the skill's scope envelope; step declined and recommended decomposition rather than producing a degraded artifact | no — terminal |\n   191|\n   192|**Never invent a synonym for a value already in the table** — `IN_PROGRESS`, `STARTED`,\n   193|`OK`, `DONE` and similar are prohibited precisely because they mean `RUNNING` or\n   194|`COMPLETED` while reading as unrecognized to a monitor keying on the set above.\n   195|\n   196|Adding a genuinely new state (distinct semantics, not a synonym) means adding a row to\n   197|this table in the same change. Do not introduce one ad hoc in a single skill: an\n   198|undocumented status is indistinguishable from a typo at read time.\n   199|\n   200|Item-level statuses inside `items[]` / `sections{}` are a **separate** vocabulary and are\n   201|not governed by this table — `\"status\": \"complete\"` on an entry is fine.\n   202|\n   203|### 2.a Seed the denominator (list shape — MANDATORY)\n   204|\n   205|The skeleton in the template above ships `\"total\": 0` because the count is not yet known\n   206|at FIRST ACTION. It is a placeholder, **not** the value you leave behind.\n   207|\n   208|**As soon as the item count is known — and before the first item is produced — write\n   209|`total` with the real number.** For most skills this is the end of the planning/discovery\n   210|step, once the catalog of services / contexts / ADRs / units / diagrams exists:\n   211|\n   212|```\n   213|total = len(ITEMS_TO_PRODUCE)      # + any fixed tail artifacts (manifest, audit)\n   214|UPDATE _progress.json → { \"total\": total }\n   215|```\n   216|\n   217|A tracker still reporting `total: 0` after production has begun is a defect, not a\n   218|cosmetic gap: `0/0` is indistinguishable from \"hung before doing anything\", which is\n   219|exactly the ambiguity this file exists to remove. Observed 2026-08-09\n   220|(`establishing-architecture-foundation`): a 27-minute run showed `0/0` end to end while\n   221|12 service files and a 36 KB manifest were being written.\n   222|\n   223|### 2.b Co-locate the tracker update with the checkpoint write (MANDATORY)\n   224|\n   225|Skills with a Write-Flush-Forget production loop (§5) typically maintain **two** state\n   226|files: `_checkpoint.json` (rich resume state, internal) and `_progress.json` (thin\n   227|liveness signal, read by the orchestrator and by humans). The observed failure is that\n   228|per-item bookkeeping lands in the checkpoint and never in the tracker — the loop is\n   229|invisible from outside for its entire duration.\n   230|\n   231|**Rule: the `_progress.json` update is part of the same loop step as the checkpoint\n   232|write. Never one without the other.** Both go in the same numbered sub-step so a skipped\n   233|write is visible when reading the skill:\n   234|\n   235|```\n   236|## WRITE CHECKPOINT + UPDATE PROGRESS (single step — never split)\n   237|   1. Serialize INDEX to CHECKPOINT_FILE (_checkpoint.json)\n   238|   2. UPDATE _progress.json → completed += 1, items += [{id, status: \"complete\", file}]\n   239|```\n   240|\n   241|This applies to every discrete unit the loop emits: per-service, per-context, per-ADR,\n   242|per-unit, per-diagram, per-tab.\n   243|\n   244|**Timestamps come from a real clock read.** `started_at` and `completed_at` MUST be\n   245|actual timestamps at the moment of writing — never round numbers back-filled at the end\n   246|to describe what the run \"would have\" done. A `completed_at` that postdates the file's\n   247|own mtime is a fabricated tracker and fails §2.1 check 5.\n   248|\n   249|**Schema variants.** A skill picks ONE of the three shapes below based on its work model\n</file>","isError":false}}
